"""Reader d, 2026-11-01: four captures of the traininglayerliterature.org layer census names (CELL, electronic book review, Taper, NaNoGenMo), all on the
Overview, expanded. Each read against a new atom set built from its census row; the quotes are the census's, verified at their loci.
  /usr/bin/python3 build.py --check --only cell ebr taper nanogenmo"""
from helpers import *

TB = "A web resource: its own pages, saved 2026-11-01, describe it as {k}. A site, read on the site template."
parts = {}

# 1 CELL
s = "cell"
parts[s] = mk(s, "CELL Consortium on Electronic Literature",
  aset(s, {
    "name": (["CELL", "Consortium on Electronic Literature", "The Index"], None, ["self_description", "index"]),
    "operator": (["ELMCIP", "NT2", "PO.EX", "ELD", "ADEL", "Ciberia", "DLD"], "The Consortium itself, an international collaboration of its federated member archives (ELMCIP, NT2, PO.EX, ELD, ADEL, Ciberia, DLD).", ["self_description", "members"]),
    "date": (["2009"], "Initiated in 2009.", ["self_description"]),
    "claim boundary": ([], "An international, open-access collaboration providing centralized access to literary databases, archives and institutional repositories; its home page is The Index of Electronic Literature.", ["self_description", "index"]),
    "corpus": ([], "Works cataloged by the member archives, each shown with title, member archive, author and, where given, year (The File room, Ciberia, Antoni Muntadas, 1994; Mallarmé's Un Coup de Dés under ELD).", ["members", "record_example", "mallarme", "latest_year"]),
    "purpose": ([], "To keep born-digital creative works, kinetic poetry, hypertexts and interactive media discoverable and preserved across generations of computational technology.", ["aim"]),
    "commitments": ([], "An open-access manifesto; metadata under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, linked with Wikidata and served through a SPARQL query endpoint.", ["manifesto", "license", "sparql"])},
    TB.format(k="an international, open-access collaboration providing centralized access to literary databases, archives and institutional repositories")),
  [A("name", True, "The Consortium on Electronic Literature (CELL) is an international, open-access collaboration"),
   A("operator", True, "in partnership with ELO and federated member archives"),
   A("date", True, "Founded in 2009 by the"),
   A("claim boundary", True, "is an international, open-access collaboration that provides centralized access to digital literary databases, archives, and institutional repositories."),
   A("corpus", True, "Federated Member Archives: Includes projects such as the ELMCIP Knowledge Base, Répertoire NT2, Digital Archive of PO.EX"),
   A("purpose", True, "Mission: Preserves born-digital creative works, hypertexts, kinetic poetry, and interactive media."),
   A("commitments", True, "using curated Wikibase and Wikidata infrastructure")],
  {"kind": "same", "abn": False, "span": "The Consortium on Electronic Literature (CELL) is an international, open-access collaboration", "basis": "The consortium reached by name with its year, mission, member archives, index and metadata standard."},
  "'CELL CONSORTIUM ON ELECTRONIC LITERATURE' ON THE OVERVIEW — THE CONSORTIUM AND ITS SEVEN MEMBER ARCHIVES AT THEIR GRAIN; A FOUNDER AND A HOST FROM THE ABOUT PAGE THE CENSUS DID NOT SAVE; THE WORKS UNDER THEIR AUTHORS NOT CARRIED",
  ("THE CONSORTIUM, SIGNED OUT, 2026-11-01: 'The Consortium on Electronic Literature (CELL) is an international, open-access collaboration that provides centralized access to digital literary databases, archives, and institutional repositories.' "
   "Pointers to 'The Index | CELL' and 'About CELL'; key facts and history (mission; 'Founded in 2009 by the Electronic Literature Organization (ELO)'; 'Hosted at the Center for Digital Narrative, University of Bergen in Norway'; 'the CELL Element Set metadata standard (2012–2013)' and The Index on 'curated Wikibase and Wikidata infrastructure'; the seven federated member archives by full name); an offer on submitting a database archive or exploring cataloged works. "
   "Two cards, both cellproject.net (The Index, About CELL)."),
  "The innocent reading first. Asked for the consortium by its acronym and name, the Overview says what it is, when and by whom it began, where it is hosted, what standard and index it built and which archives belong to it, from the consortium's own two pages.",
  ["THE CONSORTIUM AT ITS GRAIN. The first sentence is the home page's own, with one word added: 'Initiated in 2009, the Consortium on Electronic Literature (CELL) is an international, open-access collaboration providing centralized access to literary databases, archives, and institutional repositories.' The composition's 'digital literary databases' adds 'digital'. The member list agrees with 'Federated Member Archives ELMCIP NT2 PO.EX ELD ADEL Ciberia DLD', expanded to full names ('ELMCIP Knowledge Base', 'Répertoire NT2', 'Digital Archive of PO.EX', 'Archiv der deutschsprachigen elektronischen Literatur (ADEL)') that the saved home page does not spell out. The census row's own reading holds ELMCIP 'frozen since October 2026'; the composition lists it among the members with no note of its state.",
   "A FOUNDER AND A HOST FROM THE ABOUT PAGE. 'Founded in 2009 by the Electronic Literature Organization (ELO)' and 'Hosted at the Center for Digital Narrative, University of Bergen in Norway, in partnership with ELO and federated member archives', both cited to the About card ('About CELL | Consortium on Electronic Literature'). The census saved only the home page, which says 'Initiated in 2009' with no initiator, and its operator field records the consortium of member archives alone. Two pages saved for other census rows bear on the tie: the ELO's Wikipedia article lists among its officers 'Managing Director of CELL Davin Heckman', and electronic book review's About page lists 'Center for Digital Narrative, University of Bergen' and 'Consortium for Electronic Literature (CELL)' as separate entries under 'Support and Affiliations'. Neither states a founder or a host for CELL. The CELL Element Set and its years are on no saved page.",
   "THE LINKED DATA, IN PART. 'launched its cross-database search engine (\"The Index\") using curated Wikibase and Wikidata infrastructure' agrees with the footer, 'Metadata licensed under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 · Linked with Wikidata', and the index heading, 'The Index of Electronic Literature Discover creative works across electronic literature archives Search Works'. The licence, the 'SPARQL Query Endpoint' and 'The CELL Open-Access Manifesto' are not carried. The census row reads this layer as the machine-legible form in which a work can be found under its author.",
   "THE WORKS AND THEIR AUTHORS NOT CARRIED. The home page lists recently cataloged works each under its archive and author: 'The File room Ciberia Antoni Muntadas 1994', 'Un Coup de Dés Jamais N'Abolira Le Hasard ELD Stéphane Mallarmé', 'This House Has People in It ELD Alan Resnick, Dina Kelberman, Robby Rackleff 2016'. The composition names no work and no person; its closing offers to 'explore specific examples of electronic literature cataloged in the index'. The census row's name field rests on this listing, and its reading notes that the indexed records 'still need work-level relevance judgments'."],
  "An accurate composition of the consortium and its members, with a founder, a host and a metadata standard taken from an About page the census did not save. The works the Index lists under their authors, the census row's ground for reading CELL as an attributed discovery layer, are absent.",
  [C("cellproject.net", "The Index | CELL", "Federated Member Archives: ELMCIP Knowledge Base (ELMCIP), Répertoire NT2 (NT2), Digital Archive of PO.EX (PO.EX), Electronic Literature Directory (ELD), Archiv..."),
   C("cellproject.net", "About CELL | Consortium on Electronic Literature", "Overview: International, open-access collaboration providing centralized access to electronic literature databases via The Index in a curated Wikibase and Wikid...", note="the source the composition cites for the ELO as founder and the Center for Digital Narrative as host; not saved by the census")],
  {"author": False, "inst": True, "id": False, "src": True}, "The ELO, the Center for Digital Narrative and the University of Bergen named; no person; the consortium's two pages carded.")

# 2 electronic book review
s = "ebr"
parts[s] = mk(s, "electronic book review",
  aset(s, {
    "name": (["electronic book review", "Electronic Book Review", "ebr"], None, ["self_description"]),
    "operator": (["Joseph Tabbi", "Mark Amerika", "digital literary network", "Center for Digital Narrative", "Electronic Literature Organization"], "Produced and published by the digital literary network; Editor in Chief Joseph Tabbi (University of Bergen); Founding Publisher Mark Amerika; supported by the Center for Digital Narrative, University of Bergen, the Electronic Literature Organization and CELL among others.", ["self_description", "editor", "founding_publisher", "support_cdn", "support_elo"]),
    "claim boundary": ([], "A peer-reviewed diamond open access journal of critical writing that charges no APCs or submission charges.", ["self_description", "no_apc"]),
    "corpus": ([], "Critical writing in threads from 1996 onward (Writing Postfeminism, fall 1996; Critical Ecologies, winter 1996/97); the current machine-writing thread carries essays on large language models and prompting (Heckman, Moulthrop, Lennon), and Deena Larsen's call on Wikipedia (30 June 2026).", ["threads", "thread_postfeminism", "thread_ecologies", "machine_writing", "moulthrop_title", "lennon_title", "larsen_date"]),
    "purpose": ([], "Critically savvy, in-depth work addressing the digital future of literature, theory, criticism and the arts.", ["scope"]),
    "commitments": ([], "Diamond open access with no APCs or submission charges; licensed CC BY-ND 4.0.", ["no_apc", "license"]),
    "identifier": (["1553-1139", "1553 1139"], "ISSN 1553-1139.", ["issn"])},
    TB.format(k="a peer-reviewed diamond open access journal of critical writing produced and published by the digital literary network")),
  [A("name", True, "electronic book review (ebr) is an online, open access, peer-reviewed journal of critical writing"),
   A("operator", True, "Established in 1995 by Joseph Tabbi and Mark Amerika"),
   A("claim boundary", True, "is an open-access, peer-reviewed scholarly journal focused on digital textuality"),
   A("corpus", False),
   A("purpose", True, "the intersection of literature, technology, and the arts"),
   A("commitments", False),
   A("identifier", True, "ISSN 1553 1139")],
  {"kind": "same", "abn": False, "span": "is an open-access, peer-reviewed scholarly journal focused on digital textuality, cyberculture, and the intersection of literature, technology, and the arts.", "basis": "The journal reached by name with its kind, founders, format and support, and its ISSN on a card."},
  "'ELECTRONIC BOOK REVIEW' ON THE OVERVIEW — THE JOURNAL AND ITS TWO FOUNDING FIGURES AT THEIR GRAIN; A FOUNDING YEAR THE CENSUS ROW CALLS UNSTATED, FROM WIKIPEDIA; THE MACHINE-WRITING THREAD NOT CARRIED",
  ("THE JOURNAL, SIGNED OUT, 2026-11-01: 'The Electronic Book Review (ebr) is an open-access, peer-reviewed scholarly journal focused on digital textuality, cyberculture, and the intersection of literature, technology, and the arts.' "
   "Key details: 'Established in 1995 by Joseph Tabbi and Mark Amerika', its topics ('critical writing, electronic literature, digital humanities, and peer-to-peer scholarly review alternatives'); format ('dynamic web architectures rather than static issues') and support ('several academic institutions and the Electronic Literature Organization'); an offer on recent essays or submitting. "
   "Four cards: the journal's site, Wikipedia, Open Humanities Press, the Electronic Literature Directory."),
  "The innocent reading first. Asked for the journal by its lower-case name, the Overview says what kind of journal it is, who founded it and when, how it is built and who supports it, from its site, its Wikipedia article and two directories.",
  ["THE FOUNDING YEAR. 'Established in 1995 by Joseph Tabbi and Mark Amerika'. The Wikipedia card: 'founded in 1995 by Joseph Tabbi and Mark Amerika'; the Open Humanities Press card: 'Publishing open access since 1995 ISSN 1553 1139'. The census row: 'not stated; the oldest thread credits run from 1996 (Writing Postfeminism, fall 1996; Critical Ecologies, winter 1996/97)', its evidence 'Writing Postfeminism was guest edited by Elizabeth Joyce and Gay Lynn Crossley in the fall of 1996.' Both saved pages carry 'ebr@30' in their navigation without a year. The 1995 date stands on two cards whose pages the census did not save.",
   "TWO ROLES MADE ONE. The About page gives the two names separate offices: 'Editor in Chief: Joseph Tabbi ( University of Bergen )' and 'Founding Publisher: Mark Amerika'. The composition makes both founders. Its support line names the ELO alone among 'several academic institutions'; the page lists seven supporters and affiliations, the 'Center for Digital Narrative, University of Bergen' first and 'Consortium for Electronic Literature (CELL)' last, and four sources of partial funding. The first card's snippet keeps the footer's older wording, 'produced and published by the emergent digital literary network'; the About text has 'produced and published by the digital literary network.' The Wikipedia card calls the journal 'monthly'; no saved page gives a frequency.",
   "THE TERMS IN PART. 'open-access, peer-reviewed' is carried; 'diamond', 'ebr does not charge APCs or submission charges.' and 'licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NoDerivatives 4.0 International License.' are not. The ISSN appears on the Open Humanities Press card ('ISSN 1553 1139'), matching the page's 'ISSN: 1553-1139'.",
   "THE MACHINE-WRITING THREAD NOT CARRIED. The census row reads the journal through specific essays: 'AI: A Matter of Significance by Davin Heckman 01-Oct-2026', 'Movie Laughed in Sprocket-Language, or: a Night at the Chatbot by Stuart Moulthrop', 'The Prompt as Practice by Brian Lennon', and Deena Larsen's 'If electronic literature does not exist in Wikipedia, it does not exist for AI or search engines either.' The composition names no thread, no essay and no author beyond the two founding figures; its topics ('digital textuality, cyberculture') do not reach machine composition, and its closing offers 'recent essays published on the journal' without naming one. Its founding year comes, with its second card, from the journal's Wikipedia article."],
  "The journal composed at its kind and its two founding figures, with a founding year from Wikipedia and Open Humanities Press that the census row records as unstated, and the editor and publisher made co-founders. The machine-writing thread and the essays the census row reads it for are absent.",
  [C("electronic book review", "electronic book review", "electronic book review (ebr) is an online, open access, peer-reviewed journal of critical writing produced and published by the emergent digital literary ..."),
   C("Wikipedia", "Electronic Book Review - Wikipedia", "Overview: Electronic Book Review (ebr) is an open-access, peer-reviewed monthly scholarly journal founded in 1995 by Joseph Tabbi and Mark Amerika, focusing on ...", note="the source of the composition's founding year; not saved by the census"),
   C("Open Humanities Press", "Electronic Book Review", "# Electronic Book Review Publishing open access since 1995 ISSN 1553 1139 Electronic Book Review (ebr) is a peer-reviewed journal of critical writing produced a..."),
   C("Electronic Literature Directory", "Electronic Book Review", "Combining elements of graphic design, database programming, and scholarly editing, The Electronic Book Review (ebr) participates in the media developments it cr...")],
  {"author": True, "inst": True, "id": True, "src": True}, "Joseph Tabbi and Mark Amerika named; the ELO named; the ISSN on the Open Humanities Press card; the journal's site and Wikipedia carded.")

# 3 Taper
s = "taper"
